{:edb/db-before-t 2588 :edb/db-after-t 2589 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535901 50 #inst "2026-10-09T10:15:14.903Z" 13194139535901 true] [17592186147384 1020 #edb/ref 17592186373357 13194139535901 true] [17592186147385 1020 #edb/ref 17592186373364 13194139535901 true] [17592186147386 1020 #edb/ref 17592186373371 13194139535901 true] [17592186148128 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535901 true] [17592186148128 1100 "A particle of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=18\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$  The particle is also subject to a resistive force that's proportional to the particle's speed, and has a damping coefficient $c=10\\,\\text{kg/s}.$  The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with damped harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" 13194139535901 true] [17592186148128 1100 "A particle of mass $2kg$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 18kg/s^{2}$. The particle is also subject to a resistive force that's proportional to the particle's speed, and has a damping coefficient $c = 10kg/s$. The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t > 0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with damped harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" 13194139535901 false] [17592186148128 1145 "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-c{y}^{′}-ky.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $c$ gives\n\n$$\n2{y}^{″}=-10{y}^{′}-18y.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation, we get:\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+9y=0\n$$" 13194139535901 true] [17592186148130 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535901 true] [17592186148130 1100 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+7y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping.\n- ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+8y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping.\n- ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping." 13194139535901 true] [17592186148130 1100 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y^{″} + 3y^{′} + 7y = 0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping.\n2. $y^{″} + 6y^{′} + 8y = 0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping.\n3. $y^{″} + 8y^{′} + 16y = 0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping." 13194139535901 false] [17592186148130 1145 "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+7y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +7=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={3}^{2}-4(1)(7) & =-19<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents light damping, and so statement I is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+8y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +8=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={6}^{2}-4(1)(8) & =4>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement II is correct.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={8}^{2}-4(1)(16) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139535901 true] [17592186373357 1017 #edb/ref 17592186373358 13194139535901 true] [17592186373357 1089 "q-94311" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373357 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535901 true] [17592186373357 1100 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+2y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping\n- ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+7y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping\n- ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical  damping" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373357 1113 #uuid "8a56fdf8-4093-582a-8eb3-1fa47565653f" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373357 1145 "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+2y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +2=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={6}^{2}-4(1)(2) & =28>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement I is true.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+7y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda +7=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={5}^{2}-4(1)(7) & =-3<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents light damping, and so statement II is true.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={8}^{2}-4(1)(16) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." 13194139535901 true] [17592186373358 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373359 13194139535901 true] [17592186373358 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373360 13194139535901 true] [17592186373358 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373361 13194139535901 true] [17592186373358 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373362 13194139535901 true] [17592186373358 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373363 13194139535901 true] [17592186373358 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535901 true] [17592186373358 1041 "selection" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373358 1104 #edb/ref 17592186373362 13194139535901 true] [17592186373358 1110 #uuid "aeb208b6-24cb-5f43-8985-2eaf15597945" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373359 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373359 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535901 true] [17592186373359 1111 #uuid "1bcb1a67-db67-56b6-8cf2-26895aa25d6a" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373360 1034 "I only" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373360 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535901 true] [17592186373360 1111 #uuid "57dd3934-9bc5-54e5-9f11-0a86ef953b84" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373361 1034 "III only" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373361 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535901 true] [17592186373361 1111 #uuid "025f9c8c-b92a-5b9d-9560-12d7e07a2303" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373362 1034 "I, II and III" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373362 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535901 true] [17592186373362 1111 #uuid "c873a378-3273-5881-aef0-5637501c5e8e" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373363 1034 "II only" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373363 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535901 true] [17592186373363 1111 #uuid "305573ba-78aa-5688-a91d-84af92c44465" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373364 1017 #edb/ref 17592186373365 13194139535901 true] [17592186373364 1089 "q-94324" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373364 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535901 true] [17592186373364 1100 "A particle of mass $14\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=28\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$  The particle is also subject to a resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity, and has a damping coefficient $c=14\\,\\text{kg/s}.$  The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with damped harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373364 1113 #uuid "704b1e51-f6aa-5eaa-825a-515f3e83eae5" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373364 1145 "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-c{y}^{′}-ky.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $c$ gives\n\n$$\n14{y}^{″}=-14{y}^{′}-28y.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+2y=0.\n$$" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373365 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373366 13194139535901 true] [17592186373365 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373367 13194139535901 true] [17592186373365 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373368 13194139535901 true] [17592186373365 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373369 13194139535901 true] [17592186373365 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373370 13194139535901 true] [17592186373365 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535901 true] [17592186373365 1041 "selection" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373365 1104 #edb/ref 17592186373368 13194139535901 true] [17592186373365 1110 #uuid "bcb6d120-f8d3-5599-84ed-12974816e9c1" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373366 1034 "{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+7y=0" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373366 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535901 true] [17592186373366 1111 #uuid "bd81993a-d79b-58f4-854a-4c5ee4f579cb" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373367 1034 "{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+2y=0" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373367 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535901 true] [17592186373367 1111 #uuid "66496ea1-f2ed-50f4-9655-d1e677ef861e" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373368 1034 "{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+2y=0" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373368 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535901 true] [17592186373368 1111 #uuid "0028312c-8e4d-5205-ba25-90628198a2c5" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373369 1034 "{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y=0" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373369 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535901 true] [17592186373369 1111 #uuid "61ba141d-e63e-57e2-b6db-32f25c1bafa9" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373370 1034 "{y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+2y=0" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373370 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535901 true] [17592186373370 1111 #uuid "d50b4733-17ac-5392-8969-541607d84e2c" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373371 1017 #edb/ref 17592186373372 13194139535901 true] [17592186373371 1089 "q-94359" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373371 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535901 true] [17592186373371 1100 "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with light damped harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+10y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the damped oscillation?" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373371 1113 #uuid "2923e945-2143-52cb-b5fe-a0a714d9e55e" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373371 1145 "The given equation represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. We are looking for solutions of the form $y={e}^{\\lambda t}.$\n\nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +10=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +10 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9)-9+10 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda +3 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-3\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}={e}^{-3t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\n$$\n\nFrom the solution, the value of the angular frequency is $Ω=1\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\n\nThe period of the damped oscillation $T$ is related to the angular frequency as follows:\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{Ω}\n$$\n\nSo the period is\n\n$$\nT=2\\pi \\,\\text{s}.\n$$" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373372 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373373 13194139535901 true] [17592186373372 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373374 13194139535901 true] [17592186373372 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373375 13194139535901 true] [17592186373372 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373376 13194139535901 true] [17592186373372 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373377 13194139535901 true] [17592186373372 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535901 true] [17592186373372 1041 "selection" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373372 1104 #edb/ref 17592186373376 13194139535901 true] [17592186373372 1110 #uuid "66c128aa-89eb-5e48-95d3-7e6238a342dd" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373373 1034 "\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{s}" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373373 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535901 true] [17592186373373 1111 #uuid "23a754ea-72be-5685-acda-795d9b8347ee" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373374 1034 "\\pi \\,\\text{s}" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373374 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535901 true] [17592186373374 1111 #uuid "0dd8fadb-cc4a-552f-8ebd-de912e53958a" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373375 1034 "\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{s}" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373375 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535901 true] [17592186373375 1111 #uuid "254df75f-d9bf-5c29-a12d-8dfbfb4b2295" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373376 1034 "2\\pi \\,\\text{s}" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373376 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535901 true] [17592186373376 1111 #uuid "244ca7a2-2043-5ae4-929c-f91939d47b52" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373377 1034 "\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}" 13194139535901 true] [17592186373377 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535901 true] [17592186373377 1111 #uuid "feb062c5-a381-5673-820c-b0d292620888" 13194139535901 true]] :edb/tempids {"q-94311" 17592186373357 "q-94311/selection" 17592186373358 "q-94311/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6"